Package: grip
Type: Package
Title: Graph Embedding by Metric Multidimensional Scaling and GRIP
Version: 0.3.0.9000
Authors@R:
    c(person("Pawel", "Gajer", , "pgajer@gmail.com", role = c("aut", "cre")),
      person("Jonathan", "Zheng", role = c("ctb", "cph"),
             comment = "Author and copyright holder of MIT-licensed s_gd2 code adapted for the metric-MDS SGD backend"),
      person("Daniel", "Hangan", role = "ctb",
             comment = "Author of the SGD-MDS implementation in his scikit-learn fork adapted for the metric-MDS SGD backend"),
      person("The scikit-learn developers", role = "cph",
             comment = "Copyright holders of BSD-3-Clause code adapted from Daniel Hangan's scikit-learn fork for the metric-MDS SGD backend"))
Description: Embeds graphs in Euclidean space and assesses how well the
    resulting coordinates preserve graph distances. The recommended method is
    metric multidimensional scaling of shortest-path distances, defined by
    hop counts or by positive edge lengths, fitted by stochastic gradient
    descent as described by Zheng, Pawar and Goodman (2019)
    <doi:10.1109/TVCG.2018.2859997>. For large graphs, the sparse stress
    model of Ortmann, Klimenta and Brandes (2017) <doi:10.7155/jgaa.00440>
    avoids computing all pairwise distances. As an alternative, the package
    implements GRIP (Graph dRawing with Intelligent Placement) multiscale
    layouts in two and three dimensions, following Gajer and Kobourov (2002)
    <doi:10.7155/jgaa.00052> and Gajer, Goodrich and Kobourov (2004)
    <doi:10.1016/j.comgeo.2004.03.014>. Also provides classical scaling,
    scoring and comparison of layouts, traces of the multiscale construction,
    generators of synthetic graphs, and experimental refinement methods.
    Includes derived graph datasets from the 'SuiteSparse' Matrix Collection,
    with source attribution and transformation metadata.
